# Description
This creates a basic lab environment for Azure to Azure replication scenarios.

## Resources Created
- Resource Groups (Source and Target)
- Virtual Networks (Source and Target)
- Network Gateway for public access
- Windows VM(s)
- Linux VM(s)
- Recovery Services Vault


## Usage

### Setup

1. Open a terminal in this lab directory.

2. Initialize Terraform:

	```bash
	terraform init
	```



## Variables

See ./variables.tf for the full list of variables.  Here's a list of the required and commonly used ones to get you started.

| Variable Name | Purpose | Type | Example Value |
| ------------- | ------- | ---- | ------------- |
| enable_replication | boolean used to decide whether to enable replication or not for your VMs | boolean | true (default = false) |
| vm_admin_username | This is the username that will be configured on your Linux and Windows VMs | string | azureuser |
| vm_admin_password | The password to use with the vm_admin_username to login to your linux and windows machines | string/secure string | $3cUR3P@$$w0rd |
| source_rg_name | The resource group name for your source VMs and associated resources | string | Lab-ASR-SourceRG |
| source_location | The resource location/region used for your source side resources | string | eastus2 |
| target_location | The resource location/region used for your target side resources | string | eastus2 |
| target_location | The resource location/region used for your target side resources | string | eastus2 |

### Using and Setting Variables

Set variables in a local `terraform.tfvars` file:

```hcl
source_rg_name="Lab-ASR-SourceRG" 
target_rg_name="Lab-ASR-TargetRG" 
enable_replication=false 
target_location="eastus2"
source_location="westus2"
```
This file must be placed in the same directory as main.tf, or the path specified manually using the `-var-file` parameter.

Alternatively, pass variables on the command line:

```bash
terraform plan -var source_rg_name="Example-RG"
```
or
```bash
$sourceResourceGroupName = "Example-RG"
terraform plan -var source_rg_name=$sourceResourceGroupName
```

### Credentials

You can use the following to set a credential and pass to terraform

```bash
$vm_credential = $(Get-Credential azureuser)
terraform plan -var vm_admin_username=$vm_credential.UserName -var vm_admin_password=$vm_credential.GetNetworkCredential().Password
```

Do not commit credentials, secrets, state files, or sensitive variable files to source control.

### Deploy the Lab

Once variables are set, you can do the following


1. See what will be created without applying anything.  Useful for testing:

	```bash
	terraform plan -var vm_admin_username=$vm_credential.UserName -var vm_admin_password=$vm_credential.GetNetworkCredential().Password -var enable_replication=false
	```

2. Apply the changes outputs:

	```bash
	terraform apply -var vm_admin_username=$vm_credential.UserName -var vm_admin_password=$vm_credential.GetNetworkCredential().Password -var enable_replication=false
	```
This will create resources, but will not enable replication.

### Enable Replication
Change the enable_replication variable to `true`
```bash
terraform apply -var vm_admin_username=$vm_credential.UserName -var vm_admin_password=$vm_credential.GetNetworkCredential().Password -var enable_replication=true
```
**Note** - By default, the enable_replication variable is set to `false`.  If you enable replication, then run the `terraform apply` command again but don't specify `enable_replciation=true`, it will disable replication.

### Disable Replication
Change the enable_replication variable to `false`
```bash
terraform apply -var vm_admin_username=$vm_credential.UserName -var vm_admin_password=$vm_credential.GetNetworkCredential().Password -var enable_replication=false
```
If replication was previously enabled and run `terraform apply` again with this variable set to `false`, it will disable replication

## Cleanup

**Important** Make sure to disable replication (see section above) before performing the below operations.

Destroy resources after completing the lab to avoid unnecessary costs:

```bash
terraform destroy -var vm_admin_username=$vm_credential.UserName -var vm_admin_password=$vm_credential.GetNetworkCredential().Password -var enable_replication=false
```

## Troubleshooting

- Run `terraform init -upgrade` after changing provider versions.
- Run `terraform validate` to check the configuration.
- Verify credentials and provider permissions if authentication fails.
- Review the plan carefully before applying or destroying resources.

## Out of Scope
- Failover, failback, and reprotect operations
- Protection of clusters
- I'm sure many other scenarios
- This is supposed to be the most basic implementation of A2A to use for testing.  Feel free to customize as needed.